## 3. Financial Semantic Tokens — Distinct from Brand Colors

Two separate red families exist intentionally. **Do not merge them.**

```css
/* P&L direction — ONLY for numeric gain/loss, delta arrows, amount rows */
--fin-up:         #1a7a5e;   /* deep teal-green, 5.2:1 on #fff */
--fin-up-bg:      rgba(26, 122, 94, 0.10);
--fin-down:       #c23b4f;   /* deep crimson, 4.9:1 on #fff */
--fin-down-bg:    rgba(194, 59, 79, 0.10);
--fin-neutral:    #5a8a90;   /* flat/unchanged, 4.7:1 on #fff */
--fin-neutral-bg: rgba(90, 138, 144, 0.10);

/* UI state — ONLY for form validation, alerts, system errors */
--error:    var(--brand-red);   /* #ab5770 — mauve-red */
--error-bg: rgba(171, 87, 112, 0.10);
```

**Why separate?** A user seeing a "form error" red must never mentally conflate it with a financial loss. In high-stakes fintech interfaces, this prevents critical misreads.

Usage:
- `.amount--positive` → `color: var(--fin-up)`
- `.amount--negative` → `color: var(--fin-down)`
- `.impact-trend` pill → `background: var(--fin-up-bg); color: var(--fin-up)`
- `.impact-trend--down` pill → `background: var(--fin-down-bg); color: var(--fin-down)`
- Form error states → `--error` / `--error-bg`

